# Sourced, not run.
#
# The station keeps two kinds of thing, and they want different disks.
#
# One is what the station IS: its settings, presenters, schedule, pronunciations and ratings, the
# plugins somebody installed, the stream's own credentials, the recordings an operator made
# themselves, and — on one variant — the database holding most of that. It is small, it was
# authored, and it is the entire reason to take a backup.
#
# The other is what the station merely HOLDS: records fetched from a provider before they air,
# cover art, the speech it rendered, voice previews, the speech model's weights. All of it is
# derived from something else and every file can be produced again by running the station. It
# grows to the size of the library actually played, which is the only part of this that ever fills
# a disk.
#
# There is one exception on the second disk and it is worth knowing before you wipe anything. The
# PAD LIBRARY — the soundboard audio an operator dropped in — is the only thing under `/media` that
# cannot be produced again. Nothing re-fetches it, because nobody upstream has it: it is somebody's
# own files. It sits here rather than with the authored half because of SIZE, which is the one axis
# where it does not behave like the rest of that half: a drop is a few kilobytes, but the bed that
# runs under a phone call is minutes of stereo and a library of those is gigabytes, and the whole
# point of the first disk is being small enough to copy. So it is filed with the bulk and flagged
# here instead: **back up `$DEADAIR_MEDIA/pads`.** Everything else on this disk you can throw away.
#
# The pad STORE is not the same directory and is genuinely disposable: pad bytes are copied into
# `SEGMENT_DIR` like every other segment, and the boot scan rewrites them from the library on every
# start. Losing the store costs a restart; losing the library costs the soundboard.
#
# Mount something at `/media` and the second kind goes there. What decides it is whether that path
# is really a mount rather than a setting an operator also has to change: a path and a variable
# that must agree can disagree, and the way they would disagree here is the expensive one — the
# station writing a whole library into the container's own filesystem, which on a home server
# means quietly filling the disk image every container shares. Unmounted, everything stays under
# the data directory exactly where it has always been.
#
# Each variable is still individually overridable, for the operator who wants one of these six
# somewhere else entirely; only the ones nobody set are filled in here. The pad library is the one
# most likely to be moved by hand, since it is the one an operator may want on the backed-up disk.
if awk '$2 == "/media" { found = 1 } END { exit !found }' /proc/mounts 2>/dev/null; then
    DEADAIR_MEDIA=/media
else
    DEADAIR_MEDIA="${DATA_DIR:-/data}/media"
fi

TRACKS_DIR="${TRACKS_DIR:-$DEADAIR_MEDIA/tracks}"
ART_DIR="${ART_DIR:-$DEADAIR_MEDIA/art}"
SEGMENT_DIR="${SEGMENT_DIR:-$DEADAIR_MEDIA/segments}"
PAD_LIBRARY_DIR="${PAD_LIBRARY_DIR:-$DEADAIR_MEDIA/pads}"
VOICE_SAMPLE_DIR="${VOICE_SAMPLE_DIR:-$DEADAIR_MEDIA/voice-samples}"
export DEADAIR_MEDIA TRACKS_DIR ART_DIR SEGMENT_DIR PAD_LIBRARY_DIR VOICE_SAMPLE_DIR
